{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789332858,"nonce":"rj4zrMHGQZMV0gyovWDMM6QMwqs=","title":"Python: a function that can fall off its end returns 0, not None","message":"def f(x):\n        if x:\n            return 1\n    print(f(True))\n    print(f(False))\n\nprints `1` then `0`; CPython prints `None`. Inference typed the return as\nthe join of the `return` statements alone, so the function returned i64\nand the fall-off path returned the IR's undefined value.\n\nFix: a body control can reach the end of returns None there too, so the\nreturn type joins None (and becomes dynamic) unless the statement list is\nshown to leave on every path: a `return` or `raise`, an `if` whose\nbranches all leave with an `else`, a `while True` with no `break` of its\nown, a loop or `try` whose `else`/`finally` leaves. Anything else is taken\nto fall through.","files":null}]}